  <h2>Special situations</h2>
  <h3>Brain and spinal injury</h3>
  <p>Permissive hypotension does not apply: keep MAP ≥80 mmHg. Platelets above 100 ×10⁹/L, normocapnia (hyperventilation only as a short-lived rescue for herniation), and avoid hypotonic fluids such as Ringer's lactate in severe head injury. A haemoglobin threshold of about 70 g/L had better neurological outcome than 100 g/L in pooled data, though evidence is mixed.</p>
  <h3>Obstetric haemorrhage</h3>
  <p>Blood loss is underestimated because pregnancy's extra circulating volume delays hypotension. Give TXA early; treat the cause in parallel (uterotonics, uterine tamponade, surgical control) and replace fibrinogen early — a level below about 2 g/L predicts severe haemorrhage, and obstetric protocols target &gt;2 g/L. Cell salvage is an accepted option.</p>
  <h3>Children</h3>
  <p>Use your paediatric protocol: weight-based volumes, the same balanced-component principle, weight-based TXA and calcium, and a very low threshold for warming.</p>
  <h3>Non-trauma and the operating theatre</h3>
  <p>The same principles hold for ruptured aneurysm, GI and surgical bleeding. Avoid routine TXA in GI bleeding (HALT-IT). For perioperative bleeding, the ESAIC 2023 guideline supports goal-directed, viscoelastic- or lab-guided haemostatic therapy and early fibrinogen replacement.</p>

  <h2>Complications <em>to anticipate</em></h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><strong>Lethal triad:</strong> hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy feed each other — treat all three at once.</li>
    <li><strong>Citrate and potassium:</strong> hypocalcaemia and hyperkalaemia rise with transfusion volume and shock. Check a blood gas with electrolytes at every round.</li>
    <li><strong>Dilution:</strong> large crystalloid volumes worsen coagulopathy — another reason to give blood first.</li>
    <li><strong>Transfusion reactions:</strong> emergency-release blood carries a small risk of ABO or RhD incompatibility; after resuscitation watch for TRALI and TACO. RhD-negative group O red cells for women of childbearing age is preferred where supply allows; local policy applies.</li>
    <li><strong>Thrombosis:</strong> the European guideline recommends pharmacological thromboprophylaxis within 24 hours of bleeding control.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Stand down <em>and learn</em></h2>
  <p>Deactivate as soon as the bleeding is controlled and the patient is physiologically stable with ongoing product demand falling, labs are at target, and the team agrees. Tell the blood bank immediately so unused products can be returned within the cold-chain window, restock the cooler, and document every unit and time. The European guideline recommends hospitals have an institutional protocol and a quality-improvement process around it — audit time to first blood, ratios achieved, calcium and temperature, and time to bleeding control.</p>

  <div class="bundle">
    <h3>MTP at a glance</h3>
    <div class="bundle-step">
      <div class="step-label">Recognize (t = 0)</div>
      <p>Shock index ≥1, ABC score ≥2, class III–IV shock, or obvious exsanguination. Don't wait for the Hb or for units to accumulate.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bundle-step">
      <div class="step-label">Activate and communicate</div>
      <ul>
        <li>One call to the blood bank; name a single lead and a runner</li>
        <li>Two large-bore lines or IO, rapid infuser with warmer</li>
        <li>Send group and screen plus a baseline blood gas (iCa, lactate), Hb, PT/INR, Clauss fibrinogen, platelets, and viscoelastic test if available</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="bundle-step">
      <div class="step-label">Simultaneous actions</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Control the source: tourniquet, pressure, binder, packing, OR or IR</li>
        <li>TXA 1 g over 10 min within 3 h of injury</li>
        <li>Calcium 1 g after the first unit, then every fourth unit</li>
        <li>Active warming; permissive hypotension unless TBI or spinal injury</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="bundle-step">
      <div class="step-label">Blood</div>
      <p>Emergency-release group O red cells plus plasma (at least 1:2, ideally 1:1) or low-titre whole blood where available; platelets by the second round; minimal crystalloid.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bundle-step">
      <div class="step-label">Reassess every round</div>
      <p>Repeat labs or VEM: fibrinogen ≤1.5 g/L → fibrinogen concentrate or cryo; platelets &lt;50 (&lt;100 in TBI) → platelets; iCa, K⁺, pH, temperature. Shift from ratio-driven to goal-directed therapy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bundle-step">
      <div class="step-label">Stand down and review</div>
      <p>Deactivate when bleeding is controlled; return unused products; start thromboprophylaxis within 24 h of haemostasis; debrief and audit.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>The lesson that doesn't show up in any table</h3>
    <p>The evidence behind individual MTP details is thinner than the confidence of most protocols suggests. Ratios, platelets, whole blood, cryoprecipitate and calcium doses are all still argued over, and the two biggest 2026 trials were neutral. What is solid is the system: recognise early, control the bleeding, give blood rather than crystalloid, keep the patient warm and calcium-replete, and replace what the labs say is missing. A rehearsed protocol that runs at the same speed every time beats a perfect protocol that nobody has practised.</p>
  </div>

